James outlined a plan for building a "Planner," a chat-first abstraction that integrates various LLM services and tools to optimize user utility while balancing factors like cost and reliability. The system will feature a public API for registering models and tools, enabling users to easily access different execution plans. Key functionalities include synchronous chat responses, streaming capabilities, and an internal mechanism for asynchronous processing. James introduced a wrapper using a tool called BayesSelector, emphasizing a streamlined setup without complex logic. He also proposed incorporating a pre-response memory retrieval tool, allowing the system to gather context from long-term and short-term memories before responding, thereby enhancing the quality of the interactions. Overall, the goal is to create a robust framework for model and tool selection, maximizing the efficiency and relevance of AI-generated responses.
